course: dictionary
lesson: 16071
title: Wad: a thick soft lump
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one shape, and the sentence tells you which one fits. Look at the words around it. «She pulled a wad of tissues from her bag» tells you a wad is a bundle of something soft.
explain: «He stuffed a wad of gum under the desk.» Gum is soft and sticky, so a wad here is a small lump. «The thief counted the wad of cash twice» uses a wad as a thick bundle of money. It is the same word, but the clues show what it means each time.
explain: Wad can also be an action word. «He wadded up the paper and threw it in the bin.» Wadded means pressed into a soft ball. Ask yourself: is the word a thing, or is someone doing something? Then swap in your meaning and check it still makes sense.
fact: A wad is a thick, soft lump or roll of material.
fact: Material can be cotton wool, paper, tissues or chewing gum.
fact: A wad of money or paper is a thick bundle of banknotes or sheets.
fact: «Wadded» means pressed into a soft lump.
fact: As a noun, a wad has the plural wads: «two wads of notes».
exercise: «She pulled a wad of tissues from her bag.» What is a wad here? | A small sharp stone | A loud noise | *A thick soft bundle of tissues | A type of bag
exercise: «He wadded up the paper and threw it in the bin.» What does wadded up mean? | *Pressed into a soft ball | Torn into tiny pieces | Folded into a neat square | Read aloud slowly
exercise: «The robber counted the wad of notes.» What is a wad of notes? | A ticket for the bus | *A thick bundle of banknotes | A single coin | A letter from the bank
test: «There was a wad of gum stuck under the desk.» What does wad mean here? | A hard metal rod | A long thin line | *A soft lump | A bright light
test: Which word shows an action in «Mum wadded cotton wool into the gap»? | *wadded | gap | cotton | wool
exam: Ben says a wad is always made of paper. Is he right? | Yes, a wad is always paper | *No, a wad can be cotton wool, tissues or gum | No, a wad is always a loud noise | Yes, but only if it is in a bank
